A||N||1||BJW||More on an ODD engine problem . . .||||1||
Z||000000||BJW||01-26-2001||02:07 AM||||Last fall, I did some postings about an impossibly high idle leading to some code 30s and the old check engine light . . . <P>it seemed that a slightly LOOSE cold start air valve was the culprit . . . <P>but it's STILL right on the edge of being too fast and will trigger a code occasionally.<P>HOWEVER . . . a more strange problem:<P>Having rectified (sortof) the idle problem, I notice that the car engine  (after 20 minutes of regular driveing) will shoot up to 2000 rpm or so and stay there for at least 10 minutes (it seems that if I pull over and wait, it will eventually drop) . . . <P>AND . . . after a cold start, when the engine has warmed up, I notice a real ROTTEN EGGS smell from the eggs-haust (sorry about the pun) . . . could this be a defective oxygen sensor?<P>oboy . . . <P>I still love the old gal . . . ('87 V6 GT - with over 100,000 miles !!!!!)<P>She's in the garage until spring . . . so I've got time to muck with the engine!<P>B in Toronto||||reg||1
Z||000001||Oreif||01-26-2001||02:40 AM||||Idle problem could be IAC valve or oxygen sensor. IAC valve is suppose to reset itself when driving over 40mph. "Rotton Egg" smell is usually caused by a bad catalytic converter. If the cat is going (clogging) this could cause your problems.||||reg||1
